SM(182,{"n":"2 Peter 2","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Peter 2.mp3","d":2496.5,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.64,"So we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.64,"And when we start here in just a moment, we'll be picking up in the second epistle of St."],[28.72,"Peter, chapter two."],[33.2,"And I won't spend too much time going back through the first chapter or the introduction, because technically we've done the introduction a couple of times now."],[43.04,"And last time sufficed for uh chapter one."],[49.28,"Um, but just to quickly sort of cover the ground, especially what's relevant here to us starting chapter two, as we talked about uh what we're about to see in chapter two after the first couple of verses, especially, is essentially the whole text of the epistle of Jude, that we already did out of order."],[70.92,"We read St."],[71.48,"Jude's Epistle early, out of order before we started 2 Peter for this reason."],[76.84,"Um, because all the material in it gets sort of reproduced here in 2 Peter chapter 2."],[83.64,"Um, we talked about uh my at least hypothesis or theory that I've suggested, which, as I've said, is not a hill I would die on, but that uh this is because 2 Peter is actually an epistle that was put together from St."],[104.52,"Peter's teaching after his martyrdom, making it similar to St."],[104.76,"'s. Mark gospel"],[111.44,"We see that it circulates in similar circles."],[114.64,"We saw last time that St."],[116.48,"Peter even makes a reference in this epistle to the fact that he's made a provision for them to continue to hear his teaching after his death."],[126.18,"So it's even sort of implied here in the text itself that that's sort of what this text is."],[132.66,"We talked about how, despite what it says of the Orthodox Study Bible introduction, uh that's if I remember right, the Orthodox Study Bible introduction says that all of the fathers agreed it was written by St."],[146.98,"Peter. We talked about what written by St."],[148.98,"Peter even. means"],[150.78,"But uh the fact that that's not true, Eusebius, for example, lists 2 Peter with the Apocalypse of Peter, uh, as two books which some people believe were written by St."],[163.68,"Peter and some people don't, which some churches accept and some churches don't, and that's the fourth century."],[170.96,"So um there was a lot of ambiguity about it."],[175.68,"But the places that are first using and reading publicly and treating 2 Peter as canonical are the same places, namely Egypt uh and then Rome, where you see St."],[187.28,"Mark's Gospel becoming prominent."],[189.93,"Wh isich also recording St."],[191.25,"Peter's testimony after his death."],[193.25,"And so I think this is a likely hypothesis"],[196.49,"It explains everything we have in the book, it explains how this epistle is actually connected to St."],[203.61,"Peter. It's not pseudo epigraphic, meaning it's not someone else claiming to be Saint Peter, but it is someone else collecting Saint Peter's teachings and putting them together."],[221.77,"Because Saint Jude, as we saw when we talked about his epistle, was also writing from Rome, is also in the context of the Roman Church."],[228.81,"That's why you would have material from St."],[231.13,"Jude's epistle also end up getting collected here into 2 Peter."],[235.87,"That provides a pretty plausible explanation for how that happened."],[241.49,"And as I mentioned before, I find the other possible suggestions to be not as good."],[250.07,"Like Saint Peter just decided to copy, St."],[253.11,"plagiarize St. Jude's epistle in the middle of his for no apparent reason, or Saint Jude decided to take a piece of Second Peter and plagiarize it as his own like those kind of things don't make a lot of sense to me."],[267.21,"Whereas if Second Peter is collecting teaching from Saint Peter's time in Rome, and that would have been the same time, then that kind of makes sense that it would get gathered in like that."],[279.82,"Um that said, not wanting to belabor further introductory material, unless anybody has any questions or comments or news reports."],[291.66,"Yes, sir. Jude and Peter kind of operating in Cost of Aries or in the Yes."],[300.06,"Yeah, Saint Jude both St."],[301.98,"Jude and Saint Peter in the latter half of the first century AD were in Rome."],[309.18,"Um so they were operating in Rome at around the same time."],[317.11,"And their epistles sort of end up end up together."],[323.67,"The places where they show up, the places that haven't heard of them haven't heard of either of them."],[328.37,"So you don't find like a church or a father saying, well, this one's authoritative and that one isn't."],[334.37,"They either have both or they have neither."],[337.98,"Yeah. Which again is striking when you consider the overlap."],[343.08,"Like you'd think if there's overlap, maybe like one group would have one of them and the other group would have the other."],[350.21,"But that's not the case."],[351.97,"It's either both or neither."],[355.42,"So that further implies there's some connection between the two, and it's not like a plagiarizing thing or a two different forms of the same text kind of thing."],[363.87,"Okay. So we'll go ahead and get started. in uh the second epistle of St"],[368.55,"Peter, chapter 2, verse 1."],[374.95,"But there were also false prophets among the people, even as there will be false teachers among you, who will secretly bring in destructive heresies, even denying the Lord who bought them, and bring on themselves swift destruction."],[394.15,"So that but is a comparative to where we ended last time, where he was talking about uh true teachers."],[403.83,"He was talking about the apostles, he was talking about himself."],[407.05,"That they preached as they were guided by the Holy Spirit."],[411.19,"That it wasn't something coming from them as human beings, it wasn't a philosophy they came up with or a self-help program they came up with."],[419.19,"But it came from God, it came through the Holy Spirit, and so now he's contrasting."],[422.88,"He's and he says this is the way it always was."],[424.96,"He's talking about the writing of the scriptures."],[426.56,"All through history there were these men who were led by the Holy Spirit to write and teach and communicate these things."],[433.2,"But also all along the way, there were false prophets."],[437.38,"And so unfortunately, that's gonna continue to happen too."],[441.14,"There are gonna be people who come into Christian communities um who are false prophets or false teachers."],[450.74,"And uh what makes them false. here is that they're secretly bringing it in destructive heresies"],[459.5,"So when we hear the word heresy, we think while they're teaching people to believe something other than what is true."],[472.77,"So there's like dogmatic statements of the church that if you disagree with that, that's heresy."],[478.05,"But we have to remember we're talking about second half of the first century AD."],[483.51,"So there's no real church councils yet."],[486.08,"There's not like dogmas."],[488.0,"And so we have to go back to the original meaning of heresy."],[493.6,"And the original meaning of the word her heresesyy in Greek, is basically transliterated from Greek."],[496.64,"Just transliterated into English"],[500.6,"So it's the same word."],[504.02,"The original meaning of heresy was a sect."],[508.1,"Like a group."],[509.56,"Like a sect or a schismatic group or a party."],[514.06,"Like a political party or a party with a certain opinion."],[518.14,"So for example, Josephus, when he's talking about Judaism, Josephus writes his works for the Romans, but about the Jews."],[526.92,"So he's trying to explain Jewish history and Jewish customs and Jewish religion and all these things to the Romans."],[533.56,"And he talks about how within Judaism there are these different heresies, is the word he uses."],[540.64,"And he says there's the Pharisees, there's the Sadducees, there's the Essenes."],[545.77,"So he's talking about these groups."],[547.87,"But he calls them heresies."],[549.39,"And we would think we think that's pejorative."],[551.57,"But Josephus was a Pharisee."],[555.25,"So Josephus calls the group he's a member of a heresy."],[559.39,"Because at that point it didn't have that sort of pejorative meaning."],[564.29,"It just meant this is a sect or a group or a party that all holds a certain opinion."],[569.57,"Okay. So that's why you have the adjective destructive here."],[577.42,"So he's not, he's not just saying, oh, they're coming in and secretly teaching. things that aren't true"],[586.48,"How would you secretly teach things anyway?"],[589.65,"The idea is that they're coming in presenting themselves as teachers, but secretly they're trying to sort of form parties, form a sect around themselves."],[600.31,"They're trying to form their own group, their own faction, within the church."],[604.9,"To draw off a following for themselves."],[607.06,"And this is how the church fathers originally used the term, too."],[610.84,"So when they called Arius a heretic, it wasn't just, oh, you believe some stuff that isn't true."],[617.24,"It was you believe some stuff that isn't true, and you're going out and teaching it, and you're starting Aryan churches."],[623.98,"You're getting people to follow you and form this party that's splitting off from the rest of the church."],[632.26,"So that's and so while it's true that Arius taught things that weren't true."],[639.14,"And it's likely true that the people say Peter is talking about here are teaching things that aren't true."],[643.7,"That's not the biggest problem, because we're all wrong about things."],[660.82,"I don't know which 10%, or I'd fix it."],[663.64,"He said, after 20 years of teaching, he started saying, 20% of what I'm about to teach you is wrong."],[669.16,"So the same applies to me"],[669.68,"I don't know which 20%."],[672.7,"I have taught that when we get to the end of Revelation, I'm gonna loop back around and start over because I frankly think most even the ones that are available online, most of the early ones from the Old Testament are garbage and people shouldn't listen to it."],[684.95,"Even though it was me 12 years ago."],[687.67,"Um so just being wrong, just being honestly wrong, doesn't make you a heretic."],[695.13,"That does not make you a bad or an evil person."],[698.3,"But refusing to be corrected where you're wrong and going and trying to draw people off and form your own following within the church, and promote yourself, that's the problem."],[711.2,"That's where the grievous sin enters in"],[714.98,"So that's what Saint Peter is talking about here."],[718.91,"So they're drawing people off after themselves, even to the point of denying the Lord who bought them."],[727.01,"Even to the point where they're leading people away from Christ."],[731.25,"So this isn't just a question of, well, like Saint Paul talked about, uh, he said, Well, there are people going out and preaching the gospel, you know, basically to make money."],[742.97,"As a racket."],[743.85,"He said, but at least the gospel's being preached."],[748.03,"So St. Peter's saying it with these folks, you can't even say that."],[752.27,"You can't say, like, well, yeah, they're doing it out of their own ambition and to get a following and their ego and stuff, but at least they're preaching the gospel."],[760.23,"He's saying, no."],[761.27,"Like, they're actually leading people away from Christ and after themselves because of what they're teaching and how they're teaching it."],[769.75,"Notice also, though, as I have to get in at least one sorry Calvinist per Bible study, that uh they're denying the Lord who bought them."],[783.71,"Meaning Christ bought these people who are now false teachers and who are denying him."],[791.23,"Which kind of messes up the whole predestination limited atonement thing."],[796.51,"But I gotta get in one per Bible study at least."],[799.67,"So hopefully we won't run into any more."],[802.23,"But um and then notice the result of that is that they bring on themselves destruction."],[810.92,"So uh St."],[813.8,"Peter is not expressing a lot of concern for the actual false teachers, but what they're doing is destructive toward the church community and toward other people."],[824.94,"So it's worse than just hey, these folks are bringing about their own destruction through their ego and their vanity, but they're also hurting others."],[836.86,"Verse 2, and many will follow their destructive ways, because of whom the way of truth will be blasphemed."],[844.34,"By covetousness, they will exploit you with deceptive words."],[848.9,"For a long time their judgment has not been idle, and their destruction does not slumber."],[855.78,"So they're saying there's gonna be a lot of people who you're gonna see following these people."],[863.8,"And because of those folks who follow these false teachers."],[870.38,"He's saying the way of truth, which is basically what we would call Christianity, is gonna be blasphemed."],[878.01,"Outsiders are gonna see these false teachers and their followers, and because of that, they're gonna sort of slander the whole Christian faith."],[886.93,"Good thing that doesn't happen anymore."],[894.82,"And uh verse 3, then he's talking about their motives."],[898.84,"That they're exploiting the people for their own covetousness, for their own gain."],[905.26,"They're full of greed and desire."],[907.5,"And so they're going to exploit people."],[910.34,"But their judgment and destruction is coming upon them, implying if you follow after them, that's that's the way you'll be heading too."],[919.9,"Because if they're not leading you to Christ, if they're leading you away from Christ, they're leading you toward destruction."],[924.7,"Regardless of how good what they say may seem right now."],[931.75,"Verse 4 For if God did not spare the angels who sinned, but cast them down to hell and delivered them into chains of darkness to be reserved for judgment."],[942.55,"Okay, we'll pause there."],[944.87,"Um this you may remember or may not remember because it was a while ago for us, not so long for people listening to this recording later."],[953.27,"Uh this exact language."],[956.09,"We're in the part that's basically reproduced from St."],[958.73,"Jude's epistle. He used this exact example about false teachers, talking about the angels who sinned."],[966.74,"The word that's translated as hell there in the New King James is actually the word Tartarus."],[974.14,"It's the only place that appears in the New Testament."],[977.42,"The Greek word Tartarus."],[988.7,"So it's like the abyss, it's the pit."],[992.62,"In um sort of the cosmic geography of Greek myth, in Hades, there's sort of an inverted mountain, this big chasm, that's on fire."],[1004.96,"That's Tartarus. So this is, I mentioned before that uh Second Peter, the Greek it's written in is kind of aping classical Greek."],[1013.25,"That isn't anywhere else"],[1017.45,"That's why you get a word like this."],[1020.47,"But this is making a parallel between what's talked about, again, this is a reference just like in St."],[1026.31,"Jude, who made it explicit to the traditions you have in like the Book of Enoch and other places, of these primordial angelic beings who sinned and then get imprisoned in Tartarus or the abyss in Hebrew, which is like the bottom of the sea, um in this uh sort of prison house."],[1053.32,"Um, so these are not like your run-of-the-mill demons, this is not Satan or the devil, this is this particular group that's being referred here of angels who sin, and they're in chains of darkness, which is the same language that that Saint Jude uses, to be reserved for judgment."],[1072.32,"So they're there, as in the book of Enoch, that St."],[1075.92,"Jude quotes until the last day, the day of judgment, when they get thrown into the lake of fire."],[1081.84,"So this is sort of the holding tank for them until that time."],[1087.76,"So this is example one."],[1090.09,"That St. Peter uses, which is also Saint Jude's example one with regard to false teachers."],[1096.17,"And in verse five, it did not spare the ancient world, but save Noah, one of eight people, a preacher of righteousness, bringing in the flood on the world of the ungodly."],[1110.53,"Okay, so example two is Noah, which again we saw in Saint Jude's epistle."],[1118.61,"And notice some of the details here."],[1121.49,"So he refers to Noah here as a preacher of righteousness."],[1126.21,"If you go back to Genesis 6, you won't find Noah preaching anything to anybody."],[1134.21,"But if you read the book of Noah, that's part of the book of Enoch."],[1137.69,"If you read some of the other Noah traditions from Judaism in the Second Temple period, there are these stories of him going out and preachinging to the people of his time and try to warn them about the flood and tell them to repent."],[1146.93,"Of course, they didn't"],[1154.01,"They ridiculed him and attacked him."],[1158.17,"But that's the uh the dynamic."],[1162.35,"When when St."],[1163.15,"Peter here calls Noah a preacher of righteousness, he's referencing those traditions and that material."],[1170.51,"Um talk about Noah."],[1175.79,"Verse 6, example number three, which will also be familiar from St."],[1179.87,"Jude's epistle, and turning the cities of Sodom and Gomorrah into ashes, condemn them to destruction, making them an example to those afterwards who would live ungodly, and delivered righteous Lot who was oppressed by the filthy conduct of the wicked."],[1201.03,"For that righteous man dwelling among them tormented his righteous soul from day to day by seeing and hearing their lawless deeds."],[1211.75,"So this is also something that you don't see actually in the text of Genesis."],[1218.93,"But you can see how it's related to this idea of Noah being this one who preaches and receives abuse and ridicule in response."],[1227.11,"Because he's the one righteous person there."],[1230.05,"The idea is he's using Lot as another example of this."],[1234.01,"We know Lot's the only righteous person there because he gets rescued right before the city gets nuked."],[1241.47,"But he's presenting Lot living there from day to day."],[1245.76,"Having to live in the midst of this city where all of these things are happening."],[1252.72,"Verse 9. Then the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptations and to reserve the unjust under punishment for the day of judgment."],[1263.44,"So he's tying all of this back into their situation in the church communities in which they live with these false teachers and the people following them."],[1274.88,"So he's saying to them, look, I know, even when you gather for worship, even when you go to church."],[1284.67,"Sometimes you may feel like you're the only one who takes this seriously."],[1289.23,"You're the only one, you know."],[1290.59,"And then when you go out into the world in the city that you live in, people may mock you, hate you, abuse you, take advantage of you for following Christ."],[1301.87,"And St. Peter's saying, look, look at the past."],[1305.39,"This has always been true, but also look how God has dealt with this in the past."],[1313.07,"And in the past, when someone has been righteous amidst a wicked generation, God has come and rescued them. them and done right by"],[1325.35,"And all of the abuse and all of the evils that these people are doing, God is not unaware of it."],[1332.09,"And God will deal with it when the time comes if they don't repent."],[1338.41,"And so that means a number of things."],[1341.13,"That's beyond just, well, hey, take heart."],[1343.63,"God will rescue you."],[1344.51,"It includes that."],[1345.71,"But it also means you're not the one who has to go out there and try to establish justice."],[1353.23,"You don't have to try and fight back."],[1355.07,"You don't have to try and take revenge."],[1357.71,"You don't have to go and argue and try and prove all these people wrong."],[1363.0,"God's going to take care of all of that."],[1364.36,"You just keep following Christ and doing what you know you need to do"],[1365.86,"You just stay faithful."],[1371.22,"God will take care of the rest of this."],[1373.35,"Um, but you don't need to sort everything out and make everything right."],[1377.67,"God's the one who makes things right, including making us right."],[1385.03,"Verse 10, and especially those who walk according to the flesh in the lust of uncleanness and despise authority."],[1392.71,"They are presumptuous, self-willed."],[1395.03,"They are not afraid to speak evil of dignitaries."],[1398.15,"Whereas angels who are greater in power and might do not bring a reviling accusation against them before the Lord."],[1406.75,"So he's sort of describing these false teachers."],[1413.73,"So on one hand, you have these righteous people who have to live in the midst of this, and the other hand, you have the false teachers who are sort of motivating this."],[1422.13,"They walk according to the flesh as opposed to the spirit."],[1425.57,"Remember, we've talked before about how flesh and body are not synonyms."],[1431.91,"In the New Testament."],[1433.67,"Body, refers to our powers and abilities, sometimes to our physical body."],[1439.28,"Flesh is referring to our passions, our desires."],[1443.9,"That sort of come with having mortal flesh."],[1447.66,"And so there are passions and desires that are blameless."],[1451.26,"Like we get hungry, we get thirsty, we get tired"],[1457.5,"Those are passions."],[1458.38,"They make us passive, they afflict us."],[1460.46,"But they're not blameworthy."],[1461.74,"It's not a sin to get tired."],[1464.62,"It's not a sin to be hungry."],[1466.62,"Those can turn into passions, though, if they're indulged."],[1471.0,"So getting tired and needing to sleep is not blameworthy or sinful."],[1475.8,"If we become lazy, and slothful, that can become blameworthy and sinful."],[1482.95,"Being getting hungry is not a sin, but if we become gluttonous, that can be a sin."],[1489.45,"So if overindulged, it can turn into a sinful thing."],[1492.73,"And then there are passions. that are just outright sinful"],[1496.91,"Like anger and lust and right, the desire for revenge and violence and envy, pride."],[1503.39,"These things are never good."],[1504.77,"These are always like right from the get-go."],[1507.65,"Uh evil. And so when it says they're walking according to the flesh and the lust of uncleanness, that means they're just sort of following their passions and desires wherever they lead them."],[1517.58,"Their pride, their envy, their lust, their anger, whatever."],[1521.74,"Wherever it leads them, that's where they go."],[1526.35,"And they despise authority."],[1528.72,"So, meaning, here St."],[1530.56,"Peter's talking about the authorities in the church, like the apostles, like themselves, who are charged. with trying to keep good order in the church"],[1538.52,"When those people come and try to correct one of these people who set themselves up as a teacher, remember we talked about what makes you a heretic like Arius is not being wrong, it's refusing to be corrected and trying really a following after yourself."],[1552.78,"So when Arius's bishop came to him and said, Arius, you're wrong, you need to stop teaching this."],[1557.42,"Explain to them here's the truth, here's what the church teaches."],[1560.96,"If Arius had just said, okay, like yes, sir."],[1566.88,"There's no problem."],[1569.02,"With humility. But these folks, that's not their attitude."],[1573.64,"So, say Peter or another apostle or another person with authority, the church comes and tries to correct them, correct their behavior, correct what they're teaching, correct what they're doing."],[1578.36,"And they just despise that person"],[1585.6,"They just hate that person for trying to correct them."],[1589.6,"And so that's what it means by them being presumptuous and self-willed."],[1593.6,"Self-willed meaning they do whatever they want."],[1595.6,"They don't think they're under anyone's authority."],[1597.84,"And they're not afraid to speak evil of dignitary, of people in authority."],[1602.9,"So they'll go around saying slanderous things about St."],[1606.74,"Peter or St."],[1607.46,"Paul or whoever."],[1608.8,"Whoever the leadership in the church is."],[1611.05,"And then St."],[1611.77,"Peter points out, like, look, like angels."],[1616.15,"Don't go around accusing these people before God."],[1621.35,"So that's how sort of presumptuous they are"],[1627.34,"How highly they think of themselves, that they're able to go and judge, take the prerogative of judging away from God and take it upon themselves."],[1635.66,"They get to judge who's righteous and who's wicked and who's good and who's bad, and who should have authority and who shouldn't."],[1644.3,"Verse 12, but these like natural brute beasts made to be caught and destroyed, speak evil of the things they do not understand, and will utterly perish in their own corruption."],[1656.86,"So, uh St."],[1658.46,"Peter, not a fan."],[1660.14,"Not a fan of these."],[1661.7,"Um it talks about um brute beasts made to be caught and destroyed."],[1667.88,"There are some animals you can domesticate and there are some you can't."],[1672.69,"And so there are some species of animals, for example, that if they ever kill a human, like tigers, um, if they kill a human, then they will start hunting humans."],[1689.33,"And so you have to send someone to find it and capture it and kill it."],[1694.77,"That's the kind of situation Saint Peter's talking about here."],[1698.07,"Or like old Yeller."],[1699.45,"He gets rabies, he has to be put down."],[1707.78,"Yeah. Yeah. And so they they can't be domesticated."],[1711.14,"You can't turn them around."],[1712.26,"You can't get them to live with people."],[1714.26,"They're just gonna be destructive."],[1716.02,"And so they have to just be captured and killed for the sake of protecting everybody."],[1721.0,"Now, St. Peter is not suggesting that you go and kidnap and euthanize false teachers."],[1726.84,"He's just making a comparison."],[1729.56,"So there's some men you just can't reach."],[1732.26,"So he's saying this is this is the kind of this is the way they behave."],[1737.46,"They're not correctable."],[1739.56,"Because, as he just said, they won't listen to anyone in authority."],[1743.0,"They despise the people in authority."],[1744.76,"So no one can come and correct them."],[1746.2,"Nobody can domesticate them again and sort of bring them back."],[1749.82,"And get them to repent."],[1752.86,"And so they just, you know, when someone tries to correct them, they speak evil of things they don't understand."],[1759.39,"And therefore, because of that, because they won't accept correction, because they won't return, because they won't be reconciled to Christ and to the church, then they're going to end up, they're going to end up perishing in their own corruption."],[1776.27,"Verse 13, and will receive the wages of unrighteousness as those who count it pleasure to corro to carouse in the daytime."],[1786.99,"I like that they use carouse in the uh New King James here."],[1792.99,"They are spots and blemishes, carousing in their own deceptions while they feast with you."],[1799.59,"Having eyes full of adultery that cannot cease from sin, enticing unstable souls."],[1805.35,"They have a heart trained in covetous practices and are accursed children."],[1813.59,"So the wages of unrighteousness."],[1819.95,"This is the inverse of the analogy St."],[1824.67,"Paul uses. It's similar to the analogy he uses in terms of the wages of sin."],[1829.25,"He says the wages of sin is death."],[1832.55,"And when you receive wages, that's not a gift."],[1836.23,"That's what you've earned."],[1837.19,"That's the result."],[1839.37,"You work eight hours for 15 bucks an hour, get a check for 120 bucks."],[1843.85,"You don't say, Oh, thank you for giving me $120."],[1847.19,"That's the natural result."],[1848.71,"It's the consequence."],[1849.91,"So this is the same kind of thing."],[1852.08,"So when the time comes that they perish, they're going to receive the results of how they've lived."],[1858.08,"And then he compares them to uh those who count it pleasure to carouse in the daytime."],[1864.72,"So uh carousing means exactly what you probably think it means."],[1869.6,"Uh, but normally one would do that at night."],[1874.02,"It's dark, you're under the cover of darkness."],[1876.76,"Nobody knows where you are."],[1878.04,"Even in the ancient world, people went to the tavern at night to carouse."],[1880.12,"To find someone to carouse with"],[1884.94,"But this all happened at night under the cover of darkness."],[1887.66,"So someone who would carouse in the daytime, this is talking about like shamelessness."],[1893.34,"Like they're going to behave this way."],[1894.78,"They don't care who sees it."],[1896.38,"They don't care who knows."],[1899.15,"They're just doing this openly and flagrantly."],[1906.43,"And so this is again parallel to what we saw in St."],[1909.07,"Jude, them being spots and blemishes on the love feast."],[1912.87,"So they're they're they're carousing, they're they're doing this, participating in this behavior, and then they're coming and eating the Eucharist with you."],[1924.93,"And so sort of like allowing that to happen."],[1930.39,"It's like a spot or a blemish."],[1932.33,"You have something beautiful, and then there's this ugly mark on it."],[1941.74,"Their eyes are full of adultery."],[1944.14,"They can't see, they can't stop sinning."],[1947.02,"That's like they uh and this language is is picking up from again Noah's time that he used as his second example."],[1955.82,"Remember, it says at Genesis 6 that it got to the point before the flood that everyone's thoughts were always evil all the time."],[1964.3,"Like that's literally what it says."],[1966.66,"So this is the kind of people they are."],[1970.58,"They're not only sort of led about by their own passions, but they just sort of sin continuously without even thinking about it."],[1979.94,"Because their heart is trained in covetous practices."],[1982.58,"Those are the hat right, it's habitual."],[1984.66,"That's why they don't stop."],[1987.46,"Sin is just become sort of second nature to them, and so they're accursed children."],[1993.22,"They have forsaken the right way and gone astray, meaning at one point they were on the right way, but now they've left it."],[2000.26,"That's my second sorry, Calvinists for today."],[2006.7,"Following the way of Balaam, the son of Baor, who loved the wages of unrighteousness, but he was rebuked for his iniquity."],[2014.54,"A dumb donkey speaking with a man's voice restrained the madness of the prophet."],[2019.99,"So he's talking about these false teachers and false prophets, so he compares them to Balaam."],[2026.49,"Who in numbers got hired to curse Israel."],[2030.73,"So he sort of curses for hire."],[2034.89,"You could do this with Danhausen on cameo right now."],[2037.77,"You can you can go on cameo, look up Danhausen, pay him like a hundred dollars, and he'll curse people for you."],[2043.89,"Um but um that's more for comedy purposes."],[2049.61,"Uh Balaam was more serious about it."],[2052.01,"Um so he gets hired to go curse Israel."],[2054.93,"That's the money he's talking about."],[2057.14,"And so remember, these people are being led by their covetousness."],[2061.08,"Their desire for wealth and authority and power over others."],[2064.9,"To indulge their lust, sort of the way Balaam was."],[2069.18,"But the difference between them and Balaam, is that Balaam at least was able to get corrected, even if it was by a donkey."],[2078.89,"Donkey being sort of a prototypical stubborn, dumb animal."],[2085.35,"Like I almost every language, I know this is true in Arabic too, a donkey is sort of the example of the stubborn, dumb. like animal"],[2097.71,"Um, but it at least was able to correct Balaam."],[2103.33,"It at least was able to stop him."],[2105.34,"Whereas these people won't listen to anybody at all."],[2112.54,"So they're dumber than donkeys, being the implication."],[2119.02,"Verse 17, these are wells without water, clouds carried by a tempest, for whom is reserved the blackness of darkness forever."],[2129.74,"Okay, so these are images of uselessness."],[2133.68,"A well with no water in it is a hole in the ground."],[2136.64,"It's useless. A cloud that's being carried by a strong wind, isn't going to give you any rain."],[2144.14,"So it's useless."],[2145.64,"Just darkens the sky."],[2149.64,"Verse 18 for when they speak great swelling words of emptiness, they allure through the lusts of the flesh, through lewdness, the ones who have actually escaped from those who live in error."],[2163.88,"So he's saying there are these people who have come into the church, who have come into the Christian community out of paganism, for example."],[2171.98,"Paganism, where they were deluded, where they were practicing these lusts, they were enslaved by this sin."],[2178.54,"They've come out of it."],[2179.98,"And now these false teachers are coming.ing with their big fancy words and all this and lead them right back into it"],[2188.99,"Leading them right back into the same kind of bondage and slavery that they escaped from through Christ."],[2198.27,"Verse 19, while they promise them liberty, they themselves are slaves of corruption."],[2204.75,"For by whom a person is overcome, by him also he is brought into bondage."],[2212.51,"So he's saying when they come and they preach these things, they never preach like, hey, I'm going to make you a slave to sin."],[2218.75,"Doesn't that sound great?"],[2221.29,"Like they come and they say, hey, why are you following all these rules?"],[2226.49,"They come like the serpent did in the garden, right"],[2231.47,"Did God really say you can eat this?"],[2234.51,"Why is that?"],[2237.76,"You know, yeah, sure."],[2240.56,"The local apostle when he came through told you you need to not go and and participate in the festivities at the idol's temple anymore."],[2249.6,"But you know, come on."],[2252.32,"You you gotta live in this city."],[2253.94,"Like you gotta right down the line, all these things."],[2257.3,"It's always, hey, you're free, man."],[2259.14,"You can you know, do what you want."],[2261.54,"You promise liberty, but then they end up making you a slave not only to sin but to them."],[2267.71,"Because you become one of their followers."],[2271.59,"And once they convince you, now you're following them and not Christ."],[2277.51,"And to quote the Bard, you gotta serve somebody."],[2287.43,"Verse 20 For if after they have escaped the pollutions of the world through the knowledge of the Lord and Savior Jesus Christ, they are again entangled in them and overcome, the latter end is worse for them than the beginning."],[2301.27,"For it would have been better for them not to have known the way of righteousness, than having known it to turn from the holy commandment delivered to them."],[2311.67,"So these. folks are actually worse off"],[2317.95,"Coming to know Christ and then abandoning him is worse than never having heard of him at all."],[2324.99,"Why? Because as we've talked about a bunch of times, when the time comes for Christ's judgment, we're judged according to what we did with what we received."],[2335.15,"From him who has been given much, much will be required."],[2340.43,"So because they've had the gospel preached to them, because they've come into the church, because they've learned all these things, they're going to be held to a higher standard than they would have otherwise."],[2352.75,"And so that's why what these false teachers are doing is so very dangerous"],[2358.35,"It's not just that they're leading the back away from Christ, but they're going to leave them in a worse state than they were before."],[2365.99,"Before they heard."],[2368.63,"Verse 22. But it has happened to them according to the true proverb: a dog returns to his own vomit, and a sow having washed to her wallowing in the mire."],[2382.47,"So the first one of those is from Proverbs, chapter 26."],[2388.87,"Verse 11. The second one, we don't know where it's from."],[2392.71,"But as someone who has dogs, uh, I can attest that that proverb is true."],[2400.89,"Dogs will eat just about anything."],[2402.97,"They don't decide whether to eat it first"],[2403.13,"They just eat it."],[2403.37,"don't And then if they decide they like it, they spit it back up."],[2409.73,"Then more often than not, they go back and eat it again."],[2415.41,"Which is grotesque."],[2417.65,"And that's why St."],[2418.69,"Peter is using it as an example."],[2421.67,"Because that's what it's like when we return to sins that we've previously repented of."],[2431.19,"And gotten rid of. dumb in the first place to fall into sin"],[2437.85,"We ate something, we took something into ourselves, we made something part of our lives that was bad and destructive and rot."],[2446.43,"But again, but then at least there came a point of repentance where we spit it back up."],[2446.83,"We got rid of it"],[2452.18,"So then how grotesque would it be for us to go back to that again and eat it again?"],[2458.52,"That's why St."],[2459.72,"Peter's using this imagery."],[2460.92,"And then the second one is parallel to that."],[2462.86,"You take a pig, you hose the pig off, you get it all clean, it's gonna go right back to wallowing in the mud."],[2470.44,"The second you're done cleaning it."],[2474.04,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2481.4,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2486.76,"That's whole counsel at ancientface.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.64,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: 2 Peter 2 is Jude","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>2 Peter 2</b>; intro done \"a couple of times now\", last time sufficed for ch. 1</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 2 after first verses = essentially <b>whole text of Jude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">read Jude <b>early, out of order</b> for this reason</li><li class=\"d1\">Jude's material <b>reproduced</b> in 2 Peter 2</li><li class=\"d0\">His <b>hypothesis</b> - \"not a hill I would die on\"</li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter put together from <b>St. Peter's teaching after his martyrdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">similar to <b>St. Mark's Gospel</b>; circulates in similar circles</li><li class=\"d1\">last time: Peter makes <b>provision</b> for them to keep hearing his teaching after death</li><li class=\"d1\">implied in the text itself</li></ul>"},{"t":132.66,"lv":3,"h":"Authorship and where it circulated","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Orthodox Study Bible</i> intro: all fathers agreed St. Peter wrote it - <b>not true</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eusebius</b> lists 2 Peter with <b>Apocalypse of Peter</b> - some accept, some don't</li><li class=\"d1\">4th century → \"a lot of ambiguity\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First places to use, read publicly, treat as canonical: <b>Egypt</b>, then <b>Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same places where <b>Mark's Gospel</b> becomes prominent</li><li class=\"d1\">also records <b>Peter's testimony after his death</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jude</b> also wrote from <b>Rome</b>, Roman Church context</li><li class=\"d1\">→ explains Jude's material collected into <b>2 Peter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Other suggestions worse</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter \"plagiarized\" Jude for no reason - or Jude plagiarized 2 Peter - <b>no sense</b></li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter collecting <b>Peter's Rome teaching</b>, same time → makes sense</li></ul>"},{"t":279.82,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Jude and Peter in Rome","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Jude and Peter operating in <b>Rome</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">yes - both in <b>latter half of 1st c. AD</b>, same time</li><li class=\"d0\">Epistles \"end up together\"</li><li class=\"d1\">places that haven't heard of one haven't heard of the other</li><li class=\"d1\">no church or father says \"this one's authoritative, that one isn't\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>both or neither</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Striking given the <b>overlap</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you'd expect one group has one, another has the other - \"not the case\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>connection</b> between the two</li><li class=\"d1\">not plagiarizing, not two forms of the same text</li></ul>"},{"t":363.87,"lv":2,"h":"2 Peter 2:1 - false prophets and teachers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:1</b>: false prophets among the people, as <b>false teachers among you</b>; secretly bring in <b>destructive heresies</b>, deny the Lord who bought them</li><li class=\"d0\">\"But\" = contrast with end of ch. 1</li><li class=\"d1\">true teachers: apostles, himself - guided by the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a \"philosophy\" or \"self-help program\" - from <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"This is the way it always was\" - writing of Scripture</li><li class=\"d1\">all history: men led by Spirit to write and teach</li><li class=\"d1\">all along: <b>false prophets</b> too → will continue</li><li class=\"d0\">False teachers come <b>into Christian communities</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What makes them false: <b>secretly</b> bringing in destructive heresies</li></ul>"},{"t":450.74,"lv":3,"h":"What \"heresy\" originally meant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We hear \"heresy\" = believing something other than true; <b>dogmatic statements</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but 2nd half of 1st c. - <b>no church councils, no dogmas</b> yet</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>hairesis</i> (transliterated) = <b>sect, schismatic group, party</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like a political party / party with a certain opinion</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Josephus</b>: writes for Romans about Jews</li><li class=\"d1\">Judaism's \"heresies\" = <b>Pharisees, Sadducees, Essenes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we think pejorative - but Josephus was a <b>Pharisee</b>, called his own group a heresy</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>\"destructive\"</b> adjective</li><li class=\"d1\">\"How would you secretly teach things anyway?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">present as teachers, secretly form <b>own party, faction</b> in the church</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>draw off a following</b> for themselves</li></ul>"},{"t":607.06,"lv":3,"h":"Arius, and being honestly wrong","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Church fathers used the term this way</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Arius</b>: not just false beliefs - teaching them, starting <b>Aryan churches</b>, splitting a party off</li><li class=\"d0\">Being wrong not the biggest problem - \"we're all wrong about things\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>N. T. Wright</b> (stolen): \"10% of what I'm about to teach you is wrong. I don't know which 10%, or I'd fix it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">after 20 years: <b>20%</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same for him - at end of Revelation will loop back and start over</li><li class=\"d1\">early OT ones online \"garbage\", \"even though it was me 12 years ago\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Honestly wrong ≠ <b>heretic</b>, not a bad or evil person</li><li class=\"d0\">The problem: <b>refusing correction</b>, drawing people off, forming own following, promoting yourself</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's where the grievous sin enters in\"</li></ul>"},{"t":718.91,"lv":3,"h":"Denying the Lord who bought them","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Drawing people after themselves <b>to the point of denying the Lord</b> - leading people <b>away from Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Contrast <b>St. Paul</b>: some preach gospel for money, \"a racket\" - \"at least the gospel's being preached\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Peter: with these folks you can't even say that</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"out of ego, but at least preaching the gospel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">leading away from Christ and after themselves by <b>what and how</b> they teach</li><li class=\"d0\">\"One <b>sorry Calvinists</b> per Bible study\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>bought</b> them, now false teachers denying him</li><li class=\"d1\">→ \"messes up the whole <b>predestination, limited atonement</b> thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Result: bring on themselves <b>destruction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Peter less concerned for the teachers; concerned about <b>harm to church and others</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worse than self-destruction through \"ego and vanity\" - hurting others</li></ul>"},{"t":836.86,"lv":2,"h":"2:2-3 - The way of truth blasphemed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:2</b>: many will follow their destructive ways → <b>way of truth blasphemed</b>; by covetousness exploit you with deceptive words</li><li class=\"d0\">Many followers - you'll see it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Way of truth</b> = \"what we would call Christianity\"</li><li class=\"d1\">outsiders see false teachers and followers → <b>slander the whole faith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Good thing that doesn't happen anymore\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Exploiting people for own <b>covetousness</b>, gain</li><li class=\"d0\">Judgment and destruction coming on them - <b>not idle, does not slumber</b></li><li class=\"d1\">implied: follow them → \"the way you'll be heading too\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not leading to Christ = leading toward <b>destruction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"regardless of how good what they say may seem right now\"</li></ul>"},{"t":931.75,"lv":2,"h":"2:4 - Example one: angels in Tartarus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:4</b>: God did not spare the <b>angels who sinned</b>, cast them to \"hell\", chains of darkness, reserved for judgment</li><li class=\"d0\">Same exact example as <b>Jude</b> for false teachers</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Hell\" = <b><i>Tartarus</i></b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>only place</b> in NT</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek myth: where the <b>Titans</b> imprisoned - the abyss, the pit</li><li class=\"d1\">inverted mountain, big chasm on fire, within Hades</li><li class=\"d1\">2 Peter's Greek \"aping classical Greek\" → why such a word</li><li class=\"d0\">Reference to traditions as in <b>Book of Enoch</b>: primordial angels who sinned</li><li class=\"d1\">imprisoned in <b>abyss</b> (Hebrew) - bottom of the sea</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not your run of the mill demons\" - <b>not Satan</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>chains of darkness</b> - Jude's language; held until <b>last day</b> → <b>lake of fire</b>; \"holding tank\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1096.17,"lv":2,"h":"2:5 - Example two: Noah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:5</b>: did not spare ancient world, saved <b>Noah, one of eight</b>, a <b>preacher of righteousness</b>; flood on the ungodly</li><li class=\"d0\">Also in Jude</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Preacher of righteousness\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis 6: Noah preaches nothing to anybody</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Book of Noah</b> (part of Enoch) and other <b>Second Temple</b> Noah traditions: preaching to people, warning of flood, calling to repent</li><li class=\"d1\">they \"ridiculed him and attacked him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter is <b>referencing those traditions</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1175.79,"lv":2,"h":"2:6-8 - Example three: Lot","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:6-8</b>: Sodom and Gomorrah → ashes, example to the ungodly; delivered <b>righteous Lot</b>, tormented his righteous soul day to day</li><li class=\"d0\">Familiar from Jude</li><li class=\"d0\">Lot's torment not in Genesis text either</li><li class=\"d1\">related to <b>Noah</b>: the one who preaches, receives abuse and ridicule</li><li class=\"d1\">Lot = <b>only righteous person</b>; rescued \"right before the city gets nuked\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter presents Lot <b>day to day</b> in the midst of the city</li><li class=\"d1\">living among everything going on</li></ul>"},{"t":1252.72,"lv":2,"h":"2:9 - God knows how to deliver","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:9</b>: Lord knows how to <b>deliver the godly</b> out of temptations, reserve the unjust for <b>day of judgment</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ties it back to <b>their churches</b>: false teachers and followers</li><li class=\"d1\">even at worship you may feel \"the only one who takes this seriously\"</li><li class=\"d1\">in the city people <b>mock, hate, abuse, take advantage</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peter: look at the past</li><li class=\"d1\">always true - but God rescued the righteous in a wicked generation</li><li class=\"d1\">God \"not unaware\"; will deal with it if they don't repent</li><li class=\"d0\">More than \"take heart\" - <b>you're not the one</b> to establish justice</li><li class=\"d1\">no fighting back, <b>no revenge</b>, no arguing to prove them wrong</li><li class=\"d1\">\"You just <b>stay faithful</b>\" - keep following Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God's the one who makes things right, including making <b>us</b> right\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1385.03,"lv":2,"h":"2:10-11 - Flesh, lust, and authority","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:10-11</b>: walk <b>according to the flesh</b> in lust of uncleanness, despise authority; presumptuous, self-willed; not afraid to speak evil of dignitaries; angels don't bring reviling accusation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flesh</b> and <b>body</b> not synonyms</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>body</b> = our powers and abilities, sometimes physical body</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>flesh</b> = passions, desires that come with <b>mortal flesh</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Three kinds of passions</li><li class=\"d1\">blameless: hungry, thirsty, tired - \"not a sin to get tired\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can turn sinful if <b>indulged</b>: tired → <b>lazy, slothful</b>; hungry → <b>gluttonous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">outright sinful: <b>anger, lust, revenge, violence, envy, pride</b> - \"evil from the get-go\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Walking in the flesh = following passions \"wherever they lead them\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1526.35,"lv":3,"h":"Despising authority","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Despise authority</b> = authorities in the church</li><li class=\"d1\">apostles, those charged with <b>good order</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Recall: <b>Arius</b> a heretic not for being wrong but for <b>refusing correction</b></li><li class=\"d1\">his bishop: \"you're wrong, stop teaching this\" - if \"yes, sir\", with <b>humility</b>, no problem</li><li class=\"d0\">These folks: correction → <b>hate that person</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Presumptuous, self-willed</b> = do whatever they want, under no one's authority</li><li class=\"d0\">Speak evil of <b>dignitaries</b> - slander Peter, Paul, church leadership</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Angels</b> don't accuse them before God</li><li class=\"d1\">how presumptuous: take <b>God's prerogative of judging</b></li><li class=\"d1\">decide who's righteous, wicked, who should have authority</li></ul>"},{"t":1644.3,"lv":2,"h":"2:12 - Brute beasts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:12</b>: like natural <b>brute beasts</b> made to be caught and destroyed, speak evil of what they don't understand; perish in own corruption</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not a fan\" - Peter</li><li class=\"d0\">Some animals can't be <b>domesticated</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>tigers</b>: kill a human → start hunting humans → captured and killed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Old Yeller</b>: rabies, put down</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter <b>not</b> suggesting you \"kidnap and euthanize false teachers\" - a comparison</li><li class=\"d0\">Some men you <b>can't reach</b>: not correctable</li><li class=\"d1\">won't listen to or despise authority → nobody can <b>bring them back</b>, get them to repent</li><li class=\"d0\">No reconciliation to Christ and church → <b>perish in own corruption</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1776.27,"lv":2,"h":"2:13 - Wages of unrighteousness; carousing","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:13</b>: receive <b>wages of unrighteousness</b>; carouse in the daytime; spots and blemishes; feast with you</li><li class=\"d0\">Wages: inverse of <b>Paul</b> - \"wages of sin is death\"</li><li class=\"d1\">wages not a gift: what you <b>earned</b></li><li class=\"d1\">8 hours at $15 → $120 check - natural result, consequence</li><li class=\"d1\">when they perish: <b>results of how they've lived</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Carouse in the daytime</b> - \"exactly what you think\"</li><li class=\"d1\">normally at <b>night</b>, cover of darkness - even tavern in ancient world</li><li class=\"d1\">daytime = <b>shamelessness</b>; \"don't care who sees\", open and flagrant</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel to Jude: <b>spots and blemishes</b> on the <b>love feast</b></li><li class=\"d1\">carousing, then coming to eat the <b>Eucharist</b> with you</li><li class=\"d1\">spot = ugly mark on something beautiful</li></ul>"},{"t":1941.74,"lv":3,"h":"Eyes full of adultery; habitual sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eyes full of adultery</b>, cannot cease from sin</li><li class=\"d1\">picks up <b>Noah's time</b> (example two)</li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis 6: everyone's thoughts \"<b>always evil all the time</b>\" - \"literally what it says\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not only led by passions - <b>sin continuously without thinking</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Heart trained in covetous practices</b> = <b>habitual</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why they don't stop - sin \"second nature\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Accursed children</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Forsaken the right way and gone astray</li><li class=\"d1\">once on the right way, now left it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"my second, sorry, Calvinists for today\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1993.22,"lv":2,"h":"2:15-16 - The way of Balaam","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:15-16</b>: following way of <b>Balaam son of Beor</b>, loved wages of unrighteousness; rebuked by a <b>dumb donkey</b> speaking with a man's voice</li><li class=\"d0\">False teachers/prophets compared to Balaam</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Numbers</b>: hired to curse Israel - \"curses for hire\"</li><li class=\"d1\">aside: <b>Danhausen on Cameo</b> - ~$100, he'll curse people; \"for comedy purposes\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Balaam \"more serious about it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Money = same <b>covetousness</b>: wealth, authority, power over others, to indulge lust</li><li class=\"d0\">Difference: Balaam <b>could be corrected</b>, even by a donkey</li><li class=\"d1\">donkey = prototypical <b>stubborn, dumb animal</b> - in almost every language, also Arabic</li><li class=\"d1\">it at least <b>stopped</b> him</li><li class=\"d0\">These won't listen to anybody → \"<b>dumber than donkeys</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2119.02,"lv":2,"h":"2:17-19 - Empty promises of liberty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:17</b>: <b>wells without water</b>, clouds carried by a tempest; blackness of darkness reserved forever</li><li class=\"d1\">images of <b>uselessness</b>: well = hole in the ground; cloud in strong wind = no rain, \"just darkens the sky\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:18</b>: great swelling words of emptiness; allure through lusts of flesh, lewdness - those who had <b>escaped</b> error</li><li class=\"d1\">people come out of <b>paganism</b>, delusion, enslaved by sin</li><li class=\"d1\">false teachers with \"big fancy words\" lead them <b>back into bondage</b> they escaped through Christ</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:19</b>: promise <b>liberty</b>, themselves slaves of corruption; overcome → bondage</li><li class=\"d1\">never preach \"I'm gonna make you a slave to sin\"</li><li class=\"d1\">like the <b>serpent</b>: \"Did God really say...?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you gotta live in this city\"; \"you're free, man\"</li><li class=\"d1\">slave to sin <b>and to them</b> - now following them, not Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">\"to quote the bard, <b>you gotta serve somebody</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2287.43,"lv":2,"h":"2:20-21 - Worse than the beginning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:20-21</b>: escaped pollutions through knowledge of Christ, again entangled → <b>latter end worse</b>; better not to have known the way of righteousness</li><li class=\"d0\">Knowing Christ then abandoning him <b>worse than never hearing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">judged by <b>what we did with what we received</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"from him who has been given much, <b>much will be required</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel preached, came into church, learned → <b>higher standard</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ why false teachers so <b>dangerous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not only leading back away from Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">leave them <b>worse off than before they heard</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2368.63,"lv":3,"h":"2:22 - The dog and the sow","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:22</b>: true proverb - <b>dog returns to its vomit</b>; sow washed returns to <b>wallowing in the mire</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First from <b>Proverbs 26:11</b>; second \"we don't know where it's from\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Dogs eat anything, spit it up if they dislike it, <b>go back and eat it again</b> - \"grotesque\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Like <b>returning to sins we've repented of</b></li><li class=\"d1\">dumb to fall in first place - took in something bad, destructive, rotten</li><li class=\"d1\">but then <b>repentance</b> - spit it back up, got rid of it</li><li class=\"d1\">going back to eat it again = grotesque</li><li class=\"d0\">Sow: hose it off, clean → goes right back to <b>mud</b>, the second you're done</li></ul>"}]});
